<h2>Eight months in: 47 pounds down</h2>
<p>As I write this, I've lost 47 lbs — just over three stone. My blood pressure is normal. My blood sugar is back in the healthy range. I've gone from a size 22 to a size 16. But the number I care about most is zero: the number of hours I now spend per day obsessing about food.</p>
<p>I'm not naive about the future. I know the research shows weight regain is common if you stop the medication. I'm planning to stay on it long-term, and I'm working on building exercise habits and better nutrition patterns that will serve me regardless.</p>

<h2>What I wish I'd known</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Start tracking protein from day one.</strong> When your appetite drops, it's easy to just eat less of everything — but you need to protect your muscle mass. I wish I'd started focusing on protein immediately rather than month four.</li>
  <li><strong>The nausea is temporary.</strong> Don't let the first few weeks scare you off. It genuinely gets better.</li>
  <li><strong>Find your people.</strong> Online communities of people on GLP-1 medications have been invaluable for practical tips and emotional support.</li>
  <li><strong>It's not cheating.</strong> Nobody says blood pressure medication is "cheating." This is the same thing — treating a medical condition with medicine.</li>
</ul>

